{
  "query": "BlueOrbit Apps is launching a cross platform mobile service for a worldwide user base and needs a serverless data store on Google Cloud. The app persists profile documents, real time chat threads and purchase records. Many users travel offline for several hours while flying, so the client must cache data locally and automatically synchronize with the backend once connectivity returns. Which database should the team choose to satisfy these requirements?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "A globally distributed relational database that provides strong consistency and horizontal scalability.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Cloud SQL",
      "explanation": "A managed service for MySQL PostgreSQL and SQL Server that supports traditional transactional workloads.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Firestore",
      "explanation": "A serverless document database with real time listeners and client SDKs for mobile and web applications.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Bigtable",
      "explanation": "A wide column NoSQL store optimized for very large throughput and low latency at scale.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Firestore</b>.</p><p><b>Firestore</b> is a serverless document database that provides mobile and web SDKs which offer built in offline persistence and automatic synchronization when connectivity returns. It supports real time listeners so chat threads update instantly across devices. Its document and collection model fits profile documents and chat messages very well, and it can handle purchase records with transactions and security rules. It scales globally with multi region configurations and requires no server management, which aligns with the requirement for a serverless backend.</p><p><i>Cloud Spanner</i> is designed for globally distributed relational workloads and it is excellent for strongly consistent SQL at massive scale. However it does not provide client side offline caching with automatic sync for mobile apps and it is more complex and costly than needed for document oriented profiles and chat.</p><p><i>Cloud SQL</i> is a managed relational service that still requires instance provisioning and capacity management, and it does not offer real time listeners or built in offline sync for mobile clients. Building those capabilities on top would add significant operational complexity.</p><p><i>Bigtable</i> is a wide column NoSQL store built for very low latency and high throughput analytics or time series use cases. It lacks the mobile friendly SDKs, offline synchronization, and query features needed for a document centric app with real time chat.</p>",
